course: dictionary
lesson: 1330
title: Calmly: staying in control
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Calmly means in a quiet, peaceful way, without getting angry, upset, excited or frightened. Look at the words around it for clues. «Walk calmly to the exit» tells you how to move in a fire drill: not running, not pushing.
explain: The word calmly often tells you how someone speaks or acts. «Megan could say calmly, «My mum cares for my brother.»» Her words matter most, and calmly tells you she is not shouting or crying while she says them.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «quietly and without getting upset» in place of calmly. If the sentence still makes sense, you have understood the word.
fact: Calmly means in a quiet, peaceful way.
fact: Acting calmly helps you stay safe in an emergency, such as a fire drill.
fact: When you call for help in an emergency, speak calmly and clearly.
fact: Breathing slowly can help you speak calmly when you feel angry.
fact: In a story, «she said calmly» shows that a person is in control of their feelings.
exercise: «Walk calmly to the exit.» What does calmly mean here? | Without shoes on | *In a quiet, peaceful way, without running or pushing | Loudly, shouting so everyone can hear | As fast as you can, pushing past everyone
exercise: Megan could say calmly, «My mum cares for my brother.» How is she speaking? | *Quietly and in control, not angry or upset | Shouting, because she is cross | Excitedly, jumping up and down | Crying and very frightened
exercise: When you feel angry, what can help you speak calmly? | Running out of the room | Stamping your feet | Shouting as loud as you can | *Breathing slowly
test: «Saying calmly and privately «I don't like it when you do that in front of them» gives a real friend the chance to stop.» What does calmly tell you about how the words are said? | Crying loudly | Laughing and joking | Shouting in front of everyone | *Quietly, without getting angry
test: Which sentence uses calmly to show that a character is in control? | «They shouted over each other.» | «He jumped up and down with excitement.» | «She ran screaming out of the door.» | *«She said calmly, «Let's go home now.»»
exam: Ruby is in a fire drill. What is the best way to walk to the exit? | Hide under your desk | *Walk calmly and follow your teacher | Run as fast as you can | Push to the front of the line
